  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Access to education  child protection of the out of school children and their caregivers affected by the crisis in the education sector and vulnerabilities resulting from the crisis in South Lebanon</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ltpgtThe proposed project aims to enhance access to education for girls and boys, while mitigating child protection risks, in safe learning spaces, in the Nabatieh governorate, an area affected by the crisis in South Lebanon. While 70% of the initiative is dedicated to education, the remaining 30% will focus on child protection.lt/pgtltpgtltbrgtIn the education sector, girls and boys will exercise their right to education through non-formal education curricula, including basic literacy numeracy (BLN) and youth basic literacy numeracy (YBLN).lt/pgtltpgtltbrgtSimultaneously, employing a community-based approach, integrating education and child protection, parents will receive support to ensure the continuity of learning and the well-being of the children. Specifically, to mitigate child protection risks, safe identification and referrals, along with case management, will be implemented through community-based psychosocial support, focused psychosocial support, individual case management, and parenting programs.lt/pgtltpgtltbrgtOver a period of 12 months, the project aims to enable 600 children and 200 caregivers to access this integrated approach. ltbrgtlt/pgtltpgtDue to the escalating situation in September 2024 and activation of the contingency planning, the current group of&#xa0;children will continue their last cycle online with CP support online. The remaining target of children will be recruited in collective shelters of displacement from the&#xa0;South to Beirut and receive the activities as per the new guidance of the Education and CP sectors: that are recreational activities, PSS, SEL and adapted educational learning with CP case management.lt/pgt</narrative>
  </description>
